-- the decisive cell --
  multiplication, saturating, on the mirror-symmetric range [-127,127], F = 0.
  the closed form predicts associative = true (mirror-symmetric, so a
  multiplicative congruence). the naive 'signed saturation breaks it'
  reading predicts associative = false. measured: 0 bad triples,
  so associative = true.
  the closed form is right and the naive reading is WRONG on this cell.
  the geometry decides it, not the signedness.

-- the differential, which is what makes the geometry claim non-trivial --
  on ONE range, [-127,127], saturating, F = 0:
    multiplication: 0 bad. addition: 4129024 bad.
  the two operations SPLIT on the same range, which is exactly what the two
  closed forms require: mirror-symmetric is a multiplicative congruence and
  is not sign-confined, so it is not an additive one. no account that keys on
  signedness alone can produce that split.

-- the controls --
