  PASS  the predictor returned both true and false across the cube
  PASS  the two operations split on one range

== 2. the clamp isolated from the coarsening ==

  my first version of this section was confounded and its refutation is kept
  in the source. it rescaled 2F bits back to F at every step, so coarsening was
  firing the whole time and the clamp was never isolated. it measured 10890 bad
  triples at F = 2 and I read that as refuting my mechanism. it refuted the
  test.

  below, nothing is ever rescaled. scale grows F, 2F, 3F and the clamp applies
  to the exact numerator against the logical bound. the ONLY lossy step is the
  clamp, which is the clamp-alone row's own shape.

  F = 0:  no-clamp control 0   clamp, all operands 0   clamp, operands in {0} u [1,MAX] 0
  F = 2:  no-clamp control 0   clamp, all operands 360012   clamp, operands in {0} u [1,MAX] 0
  F = 4:  no-clamp control 0   clamp, all operands 1496400   clamp, operands in {0} u [1,MAX] 0

  the no-clamp control is clean everywhere, so nothing but the clamp is lossy.
  with the clamp on and every operand allowed, the law breaks at F > 0, which
  reproduces the clamp-alone result independently at W = 8.
  with the clamp on and no operand below one, the law holds at every F.

  so the clamp-alone cause has a region, and this is it: an intermediate clamp
  preserves multiplicative associativity on any operand set whose non-zero
  members are at least one, because multiplying can then never bring a
  magnitude back down past the bound the clamp pinned it to. F = 0 unsigned
  satisfies that for free, since no operand below one exists there, which is
  why my F = 0 sweep and the clamp-alone row were never in conflict.

  this does not contradict the two-causes row. coarsening remains
  independently sufficient, shown by the F > 0 columns of probe 1 where the
  clamp never fires. what it adds is the region the clamp cause does not
  reach, which the prohibition on its own does not give.

== every control: PASSED ==
